  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 291722552594432
    m_Localized: Ancient Clay Storage Jar
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722581954560
    m_Localized: 'The Ancient Clay Storage Jar is a well-preserved ceramic vessel
      dating to approximately the 5th century CE. It was discovered during a systematic
      archaeological excavation beneath the remains of a residential structure, where
      it had been partially buried in the floor of what is believed to have been
      a household storage area. Archaeologists believe the jar was used to store
      essential food supplies such as grains, rice, dried legumes, oils, and water,
      helping families preserve resources for daily consumption and periods of scarcity.
      Its large capacity and sturdy construction suggest it played an important role
      in the everyday lives of the settlement''s inhabitants.


      The vessel
      was hand-shaped using locally sourced clay before being fired in a traditional
      kiln. Close examination of its surface reveals subtle horizontal ridges and
      faint finger impressions left by the artisan during the shaping process, providing
      valuable evidence of the pottery-making techniques practiced at the time. The
      jar''s wide body, rounded base, and narrow neck were intentionally designed
      to maximize storage capacity while reducing contamination from dust, insects,
      and moisture. Although simple in appearance, the craftsmanship demonstrates
      a high level of skill and knowledge of ceramic production.


      Unlike decorative
      ceremonial pottery, this storage jar shows clear signs of regular household
      use. Wear patterns around the rim indicate frequent opening and closing, while
      traces of organic residue found inside the vessel suggest it once contained
      cereal grains and plant-based oils. Scientific analysis of microscopic residue
      has helped researchers better understand the agricultural practices, dietary
      habits, and food preservation methods of the ancient community. The discovery
      of similar jars throughout the excavation site indicates that standardized
      pottery production and organized food storage were common among the settlement''s
      residents.


      Despite spending more than 1,500 years underground, the
      jar remains remarkably intact, with only minor surface cracks and edge chipping
      caused by natural soil pressure over time. Conservation specialists carefully
      cleaned, stabilized, and restored the artifact using modern preservation techniques
      to prevent further deterioration while maintaining its original appearance.
      Today, the Ancient Clay Storage Jar serves as an important archaeological artifact,
      offering valuable insight into the domestic life, technological knowledge,
      and economic practices of the people who once inhabited the region. It stands
      as a testament to the ingenuity and craftsmanship of ancient potters and highlights
      the significance of pottery in sustaining everyday life throughout history.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148864
    m_Localized: Terracotta Miniature Stupa with Inverted-Bowl Dome
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148865
    m_Localized: "Dating / Period: 12th\u201314th centuries Buddhist Era (approx.
      7th\u20139th centuries CE)\nMaterial: Fine-grained baked clay (Terracotta)\nDiscovery
      Location: Ban Wat / Ban Jaleh archaeological complex, Yarang Ancient City,
      Pattani Province\nDescription: A free-standing votive stupa (Sathup Din Phao)
      crafted as a sacred Buddhist offering. The structure rises from a circular
      stepped pedestal with a distinct recessed waist (thong mai). The central bell
      dome is sculpted into an inverted-bowl form (ong rakhang song o khwam). Rising
      directly from the dome is an integrated conical spire formed by concentric
      horizontal ring ridges (plong chanai), which tapers steadily toward a pointed
      lotus-bud finial (bua tum). Votive objects of this type were buried within
      brick sanctuary foundations to generate religious merit."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148866
    m_Localized: Tiered Umbrella Spire of a Miniature Terracotta Stupa
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148867
    m_Localized: "Period / Dating: 12th\u201314th centuries Buddhist Era (approx.
      7th\u20139th centuries CE)\nMaterial: Fine-grained baked earthenware / Terracotta
      (Din Phao Nuea La-iat)\nDimensions: Total Height: 46 cm | Base Diameter (Bottom
      Plate): 16.7 cm\nProvenance / Discovery Site: Ban Jaleh Monument Complex, Yarang
      Ancient City, Pattani Province (Surveyed by Anan Wattananikorn, B.E. 2505\u20132516
      / 1962\u20131973)\nCurrent Repository: Princess Galyani Vadhana Institute of
      Cultural Studies, Prince of Songkla University, Pattani Campus (Catalog Item
      23)\n\nCuratorial Description\nThis modular baked clay artifact represents
      an authentic chatra-valee (tiered umbrella spire) crafted to crown the superstructure
      of a miniature terracotta votive stupa (Sathup Din Phao).\n\nThe spire is constructed
      from 7 concentric, ring-shaped umbrella plates (chatra) engineered with raised
      central cylindrical sleeves. These interlocking components are stacked vertically
      along a central axis in decreasing diameter from the wide base plate to the
      narrow summit, forming an elegant graduated conical silhouette. The pinnacle
      terminates in a tall, elongated pointed lotus-bud finial (bua tum song sung).\n\nArchaeological
      & Spiritual Significance\nDiscovered within the ancient architectural complex
      of Ban Jaleh, this artifact showcases the specialized modular ceramic production
      techniques developed in the Yarang workshops during the height of the ancient
      maritime kingdom of Langkasuka.\n\nIn Buddhist cosmology, the multi-tiered
      chatra-valee symbolizes the ascending celestial realms (heavens) and royal-spiritual
      sovereignty. Miniature stupas crowned with these multi-tiered spires were produced
      in vast numbers as devotional merit-making offerings and consecrated foundation
      deposits, reflecting the widespread presence of Mahayana Buddhism and the Cetiyavada
      school in early peninsular Southeast Asia."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148868
    m_Localized: Standing Stone Buddha in Vitarka Mudra
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148869
    m_Localized: "- Period / Dating: 13th-14th centuries Buddhist Era (c. 8th-9th
      centuries CE)\n- Material: Carved stone (Sandstone)\n- Dimensions: Height:
      60 cm | Width: 16 cm\n- Provenance: Ban Kampong Baru (present-day Ban Jaleh),
      Yarang Ancient City, Pattani Province\n- Current Repository: Unknown / Unrecorded\n\nCuratorial
      Description:\nThis monolithic stone sculpture depicts a standing Buddha in
      a rigid upright frontal posture (samabhanga) resting atop an open double-lotus
      pedestal (bua khwam bua ngai). Both hands are raised symmetrically to perform
      the Vitarka Mudra\u2014the gesture of teaching, debate, and intellectual exposition.
      The Buddha is draped in a diaphanous, sheer monastic robe (civara) covering
      both shoulders (hom khlum) that clings tightly to the body contours. The frontal
      hem of the mantle forms a broad U-shaped curve extending down to the shins,
      revealing an inner lower garment that falls to the ankles.\n\nFacial and anatomical
      treatments reflect distinct regional modeling, characterized by a flat facial
      plane, downcast meditative eyes, wide lips, bold spiral hair curls, and a rounded,
      cylindrical ushnisha (cranial protuberance).\n\nWhile the image shares stylistic
      affinities with Southern peninsular sculptures from Wat Kaeo and Ban U-thae
      (Surat Thani) as well as Central Thai Dvaravati traditions, it demonstrates
      a distinctive innovation unique to the ancient Yarang workshops: both hands
      are carved resting directly against the lower abdomen at navel level, firmly
      attached to the torso rather than raised independently at chest or waist height.
      This piece stands as vital physical evidence of the refined local craftsmanship
      and Mahayana Buddhist devotional networks flourishing in the ancient maritime
      kingdom of Langkasuka."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148870
    m_Localized: Architectural Terracotta Stupa Base with Kudu Niches
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148871
    m_Localized: '- Dating / Period: 12th-14th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 7th-9th
      centuries CE)

      - Dimensions: Diameter: 11.50 cm | Height: 16.50 cm

      -
      Material: Baked clay with applied relief and red mineral pigment traces

      -
      Discovery Location: Excavated at Ancient Monument Mound No. 3, Ban Jaleh, Yarang
      Ancient City, Pattani Province

      - Current Repository: Fine Arts Department
      Office No. 11, Songkhla

      - Description: A cylindrical architectural stupa
      base featuring decorative details created through the applied clay technique
      (pan-pae). The lower rim is bordered by a tooth/gear frieze supporting alternating
      blind horseshoe-arch niches (kudu) flanked by slender rounded pilasters. Adjacent
      panels display blind rectangular windows fitted with vertical wooden-style
      balustrade bars (lak krong mai). The upper register is crowned with false projecting
      roof rafters (khue), four-petaled floral rosettes, and an upward-lotus moulding
      that originally supported a dome and tiered spire. This artifact illustrates
      early Dvaravati-Gupta architectural styles replicated on a miniature scale.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148872
    m_Localized: Tripartite Stone Shiva Linga
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148873
    m_Localized: '- Period / Dating: c. 7th-8th Century CE (12th-13th Centuries Buddhist
      Era)

      - Material: Carved sandstone

      - Dimensions: Height: 58 cm
      | Width: 16 cm | Thickness: 16 cm

      - Discovery Location: Western moat
      area, Ban Prawae Ancient Monument Group, Yarang District, Pattani Province
      (Found in 1977)

      - Current Repository: Art and Cultural Center, Yala
      Rajabhat University


      Curatorial Description:

      This monolithic
      sandstone sculpture is a classic canonical (bap prapheni-niyom) Shiva linga
      sculpted into three equal vertical sections symbolizing the Hindu Trimurti:


      -
      Rudra-bhaga (Top): A smooth, egg-shaped cylinder representing Lord Shiva, carved
      with a vertical central ridge (Brahmasutra) and curved lateral contour lines
      (Parsvasutra) extending toward the rear.

      - Vishnu-bhaga (Middle): An
      octagonal prismatic shaft symbolizing Lord Vishnu, designed to sit within a
      sacred yoni pedestal.

      - Brahma-bhaga (Base): A square prismatic block
      representing Lord Brahma, intended to be anchored into the sanctuary foundation.


      The
      discovery of this artifact provides key material evidence of active Shaivite
      Hindu worship and syncretic religious culture flourishing alongside Buddhism
      within the ancient maritime entrepot of Yarang (Langkasuka).'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148874
    m_Localized: Stone Sculpture of a Recumbent Bull / Nandi
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148875
    m_Localized: "- Period / Dating: 11th-12th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 6th-7th
      centuries CE)\n- Material: Carved sandstone (Hin Sai)\n- Dimensions: Height:
      52 cm | Length: 66 cm | Width: 29.5 cm\n- Discovery Location: Excavated from
      Ancient Monument No. 8, Ban Jaleh, Yarang Ancient City, Pattani Province\n-
      Current Repository: Songkhla National Museum\n\nCuratorial Description:\nDiscovered
      beside an interior brick pedestal within an 8x8 meter pillared sanctuary hall
      at Ban Jaleh Monument No. 8, this monolithic sandstone sculpture portrays a
      humped zebu bull (tha non mop) resting in a tranquil, recumbent posture with
      its front legs folded beneath its chest. The sculpture features smooth, minimalist
      surfaces devoid of ornamental harnesses or jewelry, emphasizing the bull's
      naturalistic anatomy, upright ears, short horns, and a pronounced dorsal hump
      rising above the shoulders.\n\nIn ancient South and Southeast Asian art, the
      bull holds dual religious significance:\n- Hindu / Shaivite Iconography: The
      bull represents Nandi (Kho Nonthi), the sacred vehicle (vahana) and divine
      gatekeeper of Lord Shiva. Sculptures of Nandi are traditionally placed near
      sanctuary entrances facing inward toward the central linga.\n- Buddhist Cosmology
      & Lineage: The bull embodies Gotama (referencing Shakyamuni Buddha's lineage
      name) and serves as the Guardian Animal of the Western Direction. This directional
      motif draws directly from early Indian traditions\u2014such as Emperor Ashoka\u2019s
      pillar capitals featuring the elephant (East), horse (South), bull (West),
      and lion (North)\u2014as well as the carved semicircular moonstones (Sandakada
      Pahana) of Sri Lanka's Anuradhapura period (4th-16th centuries BE).\n\nThe
      discovery of sandstone chisel debitage alongside a bronze Avalokiteshvara head
      near the sanctuary base indicates that this monolithic sculpture was carved
      locally, standing as physical evidence of the syncretic Hindu-Buddhist devotional
      culture that thrived within the ancient maritime entrepot of Langkasuka."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148876
    m_Localized: Terracotta Turtle-Shaped Vessel Lid
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148877
    m_Localized: '- Period / Dating: 12th-16th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 7th-11th
      centuries CE)

      - Material: Baked earthenware / Terracotta (Din Phao)

      -
      Dimensions: Diameter: 18.2 cm | Height: 4.7 cm

      - Discovery Location:
      Excavated from the archaeological site at Ban Jaleh, Yarang Ancient City, Pattani
      Province

      - Current Repository: Regional Office of Fine Arts No. 11,
      Songkhla


      Curatorial Description:

      Sculpted from local baked earthenware,
      this circular lid is modeled in the naturalistic form of a flattened turtle
      (Fa Phachana Din Phao Rup Tao). The piece features a broad domed carapace,
      four short protruding feet scored with five linear incisions each to denote
      individual toes, and a protruding turtle head extending forward with a subtle
      tongue-out expression.


      The upper surface of the carapace bears clear
      archaeological evidence of applied sculptural ornamentation:

      - Miniature
      Sculptures: Two distinct scar marks on the upper shell mark where miniature
      baby turtle figurines were originally affixed before detaching over time.

      -
      Rear Ornamentation: An additional scar mark at the rear edge indicates the
      original placement of another small decorative finial or miniature element
      of unidentified form.


      Ritual & Cultural Significance:

      Archaeological
      analysis indicates that this zoomorphic lid was specifically crafted to seal
      a consecrated earthenware deposit vessel containing auspicious items and sacred
      relics (watthu mongkhon). Such vessels were deposited within the structural
      foundations of religious monuments during auspicious groundbreaking and consecration
      rituals (kan wang roek).


      In ancient South and Southeast Asian cosmology,
      the turtle embodies foundational stability, cosmic support, and protection
      against negative energies. Discovered within the monument complex of Ban Jaleh,
      this artifact provides tangible material evidence of the intricate foundation-laying
      rituals and skilled ceramic craftsmanship practiced within the ancient peninsular
      trade kingdom of Langkasuka.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148878
    m_Localized: Bronze Statue of the Sun God Surya
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 291722586148879
    m_Localized: "- Period / Dating: 15th-16th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 10th-11th
      centuries CE)\n- Material: Cast bronze\n- Dimensions: Height: 20.30 cm | Width:
      10.30 cm\n- Discovery Location: Ban Kuwing, Sakho Bon Sub-district, Mayo District,
      Pattani Province (approx. 7 km east of Yarang Ancient City)\n- Current Repository:
      Unknown / Unrecorded\n\nCuratorial Description:\nCast in bronze, this intricate
      sculptural triad depicts Surya, the Hindu Sun God and supreme deity of the
      Saura sect. The composition features Surya standing in a rigid upright frontal
      pose (samabhanga) atop a lotus pedestal mounted over his celestial single-wheeled
      chariot. The chariot is drawn by seven galloping horses\u2014with the central
      steed emerging directly from the wheel's hub\u2014and is steered by the divine
      dawn charioteer, Aruna.\n\nSurya is flanked by his celestial retinue and personifications
      of dawn:\n- Pingala & Goddess Usha (Right): The attendant Pingala stands in
      a graceful dvibhanga posture holding a writing implement and an inkpot, fronted
      by a relief image of the dawn goddess Usha, the dispeller of darkness.\n- Danda
      & Goddess Pratyusha (Left): The attendant Danda stands in a dvibhanga pose
      holding a ritual attribute (possibly a conch), fronted by the partially preserved
      form of Goddess Pratyusha.\n- Regalia & Attributes: Surya wears a tall conical
      jewel crown (Kiritamukuta), a bare upper torso, fitted trousers (sanapphlao),
      and knee-high riding boots. Both hands are raised at hip level to hold stemmed
      lotus bouquets that rise symmetrically to the level of his head.\n- Aureole
      & Royal Canopy: The grouping is framed by an openwork arched halo (prabhavali)
      bordered with bead moulding and stylized leaf-like flame rays, surmounted at
      the peak by a royal tiered parasol (chattr) draped with fluttering banners
      and garlands.\n\nArtistic & Cultural Significance:\nStylistically, this sculpture
      exhibits strong affinities with the 10th-11th century CE Pala-period art of
      Northeast India (Bengal). Discovered in the immediate vicinity of Yarang Ancient
      City, the artifact provides key archaeological evidence of the active practice
      of the Saura tradition and highlights the extensive maritime trade and religious
      networks connecting the Indian subcontinent with the ancient peninsular kingdom
      of Langkasuka."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 297797452484608
    m_Localized: Bronze Bodhisattva Maitreya
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 297797481844736
    m_Localized: "- Period / Dating: 12th-13th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 7th-8th
      centuries CE)\n- Material: Cast bronze (lost-wax technique)\n- Dimensions:
      Height: 16.2 cm\n- Discovery Location: Ban Lan Khwai (Moo 5, Kolam Sub-district),
      Yarang District, Pattani Province\n- Current Repository: Bangkok National Museum
      (Phra Nakhon National Museum)\n\nCuratorial Description:\nCast in bronze using
      the traditional lost-wax (cire perdue) technique, this statuette portrays Bodhisattva
      Maitreya, the future Buddha who resides in Tusita Heaven awaiting descent to
      the earthly realm following Gautama Buddha. The deity is sculpted in a graceful,
      slender tribhanga (triple-bend) stance that imparts a fluid, naturalistic rhythm
      to the torso and hips. Although both arms are broken off and missing below
      the shoulders, the preserved body contours retain fine anatomical balance.\n\nThe
      Bodhisattva's hair is swept up into a tall, tiered ascetic crown of matted
      locks (jatamukuta), adorned at the front center with a miniature stupa\u2014the
      primary iconographic symbol identifying Maitreya. A sacred cord (yajnopavita)
      drapes diagonally over the left shoulder across the chest, while a lower skirt-cloth
      (sabong) wraps around the hips, fastened securely at the waist with a tied
      sash (ratprakot).\n\nStylistically, the modeling of the tiered hairstyle and
      the clinging drapery of the lower garment exhibits strong affinities with South
      Indian and Sri Lankan sculptural traditions. Discovered in the Yarang archaeological
      sphere, this statuette serves as significant material evidence of the sophisticated
      metallurgy, maritime cross-cultural exchange, and Buddhist devotional culture
      that flourished within the ancient kingdom of Langkasuka."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 297797481844737
    m_Localized: Stone Dharmachakra / Wheel of the Law
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 297797481844738
    m_Localized: '- Period / Dating: 11th-12th centuries Buddhist Era (approx. 6th-7th
      centuries CE)

      - Material: Carved sandstone (Hin Sai)

      - Dimensions:
      Height: 33.5 cm | Wheel Diameter: 22.5 cm | Hub Diameter: 6 cm

      - Discovery
      Location: Ban Wat Ancient Monument Mound No. 25 (corresponding to Mound No.
      6 today), Yarang Ancient City, Pattani Province

      - Current Repository:
      Princess Galyani Vadhana Institute of Cultural Studies, Prince of Songkla University,
      Pattani Campus


      Curatorial Description:

      Carved from dense sandstone,
      this monolithic Dharmachakra (Wheel of the Law) represents an early aniconic
      tradition in Buddhist art established prior to the depiction of the Buddha
      in anthropomorphic form. The wheel symbolizes the First Sermon (Dhammacakkappavattana
      Sutta, or the "Turning of the Wheel of the Dhamma") delivered by the Buddha
      at the Deer Park in Isipatana near Varanasi.


      The sculpture features
      a circular wheel with 8 openwork spokes radiating outward from a smooth, unadorned
      circular hub. Structurally, the wheel is mounted directly atop a solid rectangular
      stone block, which rests on a rounded, flattened circular base pedestal.


      This
      piece shares notable stylistic and iconographic parallels with early stone
      Dharmachakras found at Oc Eo in Vietnam and the Khao Phra Narai (Khao Siwichai)
      archaeological site in Phunphin District, Surat Thani Province. Its presence
      at Yarang provides vital archaeological evidence of the region''s active participation
      in early trans-peninsular trade networks and its significance as a major Buddhist
      center within maritime Southeast Asia during the pre-Dvaravati and early Dvaravati
      eras.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 298432247812096
    m_Localized: Bronze Bodhisattva Maitreya
    m_Metadata:
